messages:
ravi: Heads-up: multiple new users hitting import/runtime errors on the JS SDK during onboarding. Quickstart shows one pattern and it breaks in several setups (CJS, ESM, TS, edge).
jen: Yep. Errors we're seeing: "Named export 'InferenceClient' not found" on `import { InferenceClient } ...`, or `TypeError: Cannot read property 'InferenceClient' of undefined` from require(). Also a few reports from Bun/Deno users.
support-bot: Collected samples from support tickets and pasted into the internal triage doc. Most are copy/paste errors from the quickstart.
jen: Proposal: add a tiny "Quick import cheat-sheet" to the SDK README with exact copy/paste blocks for common runtimes + a short troubleshooting section. Folks want one-liners that work.
jen: Suggested snippets (short):
```
// Node CommonJS (package.json WITHOUT "type": "module")
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new (Redwood.InferenceClient || Redwood.default?.InferenceClient)({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// Node ESM ("type": "module" or .mjs) - default import
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood.InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// ESM preferred / Edge runtimes (named import)
import { InferenceClient } from '@redwood/inference';
const client2 = new InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// TypeScript (recommended tsconfig shown below)
// If you can't enable esModuleInterop, use the TS import=require() fallback
import Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const clientTs = new (Redwood as any).InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
```
jen: Tiny tsconfig suggestion to include in docs: `{"compilerOptions": {"esModuleInterop": true, "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true}}`
ravi: Also add a short note about "why this happens": bundlers/Node choose entries based on package.json keys and the package's export layout. If a package only exposes a default ESM export, then `import { Named }` will fail and `require()` may return a namespace with `.default`.
jen: Add a short package.json exports example for advanced users so they can understand conditional exports:
```json
{
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
      "require": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs"
    }
  },
  "main": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs",
  "module": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
}
```
jen: Also include quick debug commands users/support can run to see what they actually installed: `node -e "console.log(require('@redwood/inference'))"` and `node -p "import('@redwood/inference').then(m=>console.log(m))"` (in an ESM shell). That helps triage whether they got a default-only export.
support-bot: I'll add a support playbook snippet that links to these commands and the new README section.
build-eng: Operational items: INF-342 created to normalize publish outputs (dist/cjs + dist/esm + matching .d.ts) and add an exports map in package.json. We'll also add CI smoke tests that run tiny examples for each target (node-cjs, node-esm, tsc compile, and an edge dev run).
jen: Short term: docs + examples reduce customer friction fastest. I'll draft a PR with a short "CJS / ESM / TS / Edge" section and tiny example apps under examples/js/{cjs,esm,ts,edge}. Each example will include exact npm/node commands and expected output.
docs-bot: Draft PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/585 - please review the cheat-sheet and examples links.
ravi: One more: call out runtimes explicitly that are ESM-only (Vercel Edge, Cloudflare Workers, Deno). Example: 
```
// Vercel Edge handler (ESM-only)
import { InferenceClient } from '@redwood/inference';
export default async function handler(req) {
  const c = new InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
  const out = await c.generate({ prompt: 'hi' });
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(out));
}
```
jen: After docs land we'll update the onboarding checklist and the quickstart templates used by sign-up flows so new users get the right snippet for their environment.
support-bot: Linking INF-342 in support playbook so escalations reference the normalization ticket.
build-eng: We'll prioritize the publish normalization RFC after the current sprint; for now docs + examples + support playbook should cut the ticket volume.
ravi: Thanks all. Landing docs PR and updating onboarding checklist are the immediate next steps. Closing this thread when PR is merged.
